  "notes": {
    "noteA": {
      "fill": "gray",
      "page": "styles",
      "size": 24,
      "text": "Instrument — chosen by the user on 4 Oct 2026.\n\nInstrument\n\nMonospaced tabular numbers, hairline rules, square corners, one signal orange. Reads like lab equipment, which fits an app built on raw packets and post-hoc analysis.\n\nBest at: dense data (probe, history, charts) and staying calm on every screen.\nRisk: can feel cold. The live numbers rely on size, not colour.\nFonts: SF Mono and SF Pro on iPhone (0 kB). Elsewhere IBM Plex.\nTweaks: dark mode, progress ruler.",
      "w": 380,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 0
    },
    "noteB": {
      "fill": "orange",
      "page": "styles",
      "size": 24,
      "text": "Crema\n\nDark-roast background, crema-gold accent, rounded numerals, soft cards, a ring gauge for the shot. Dark first, so it's easy on the eyes at 6 am.\n\nBest at: feeling like a coffee product you want to open every morning.\nRisk: brown on brown needs contrast discipline. The ring takes space that the numbers could use.\nFonts: SF Rounded on iPhone (0 kB). Elsewhere Nunito.\nTweaks: light mode, ring gauge.",
      "w": 380,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 1300
    },
    "noteC": {
      "fill": "blue",
      "page": "styles",
      "size": 24,
      "text": "Native\n\nGrouped inset lists, large titles, segmented controls and switches. Follows iOS conventions so closely it could be a built-in app.\n\nBest at: zero learning curve, cheapest to build and keep consistent, and it suits the 'rudimentary until T3.5' rule.\nRisk: no identity of its own, and it looks off on desktop Chrome.\nFonts: SF Pro (system, 0 kB).\nTweaks: dark mode, progress bar.",
      "w": 380,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 2600
    },
    "noteD": {
      "fill": "purple",
      "page": "styles",
      "size": 24,
      "text": "Signal\n\nGiant condensed numerals on black. The screen fills from the bottom like the cup as the shot nears its target. The direction tap uses three huge colour blocks, easy to hit with wet hands.\n\nBest at: the spec's peripheral-vision rule and the two-tap rule.\nRisk: loud on the calmer screens (history, probe), and it needs a webfont (about 15 kB, digits subset).\nFonts: Barlow Condensed.\nTweaks: light mode, screen fill.",
      "w": 380,
      "x": 1410,
      "y": 3900
    },
    "titleA": {
      "kind": "title1",
      "maxW": 1330,
